1. Who may use the Software
   Only a person or organization that has signed a Customer Agreement with
   the licensor (the "Customer"). The Customer Agreement names the number of
   deployments, the start date and the price. If the Customer
   Agreement and this license disagree, the Customer Agreement wins.

2. What the Customer may do
   (a) Install and run the Software on the number of deployments the
       Customer Agreement states. A "deployment" is one running installation
       with its own database, whatever number of servers it uses.
   (b) Read, change and extend the source code, and build its own products
       on it.
   (c) Operate those products for its own business and offer them to its
       own customers and their users, hosted or on its own premises,
       including selling plans and features to its tenants.
   (d) Let its employees and contractors work on the Software for the
       Customer, bound by obligations at least as strict as these.

3. Versions and updates
   (a) The right to use every version delivered to the Customer is
       perpetual, subject to section 6.
   (b) The Customer receives the new versions the licensor releases during
       the update period stated in the Customer Agreement (12 months unless
       it says otherwise). A renewal extends the period. When it ends,
       delivery of new versions stops; the versions already delivered keep
       working.

4. What the Customer may not do
   (a) Give, sell, sublicense, publish or otherwise make available the
       Software or any substantial part of its source code to anyone else,
       changed or unchanged, except as running software inside the products
       of section 2(c).
   (b) Sell, license or offer the Software, or a product whose main purpose
       is to be a starter kit, boilerplate, framework or platform for
       building other software, in competition with the Software.
   (c) Use it on more deployments than licensed.
   (d) Remove or change the copyright and license notices.
   (e) Remove, disable or work around license-checking functionality the
       Software contains, where the Customer's license makes it apply.

5. Ownership
   The licensor keeps all rights in the Software that this license does not
   expressly grant. Changes and new works the Customer makes belong to the
   Customer, except for the parts of the Software they contain.

6. Termination
   If the Customer breaches this license or the Customer Agreement and does
   not cure the breach within 30 days of written notice, the licenses end
   and the Customer must stop using and delete the Software. Products the
   Customer's own customers rely on may be wound down within 90 days.
   Ending the update period is not a termination.

